<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>A credential value originating from <code>KyotoUAuth</code> reaches a
sink that persists it to disk or to a serialization buffer:
<code>open().write</code>, <code>pathlib.Path.write_text</code>,
<code>json.dump</code> / <code>json.dumps</code>,
<code>pickle.dump</code> / <code>pickle.dumps</code>, or a
<code>csv.writer</code> row. The kuauth library does not own a credential
store and must never write the user's password or TOTP secret to a
file.</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>Credentials are inputs to the library, not outputs. If the user wants
persistence (e.g. for unattended cron jobs), they configure
<code>totp_secret</code> at construction time and the library reads it
into memory. Do not introduce caches, debug dumps, or HAR captures that
include credential fields.</p>

<p>If you legitimately need to record an HTTP exchange for replay tests,
strip the <code>password</code> / <code>j_password</code> / OTP fields
from the captured form data before writing the HAR. The
<code>tests/replay</code> fixtures in this repo do exactly that.</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>Bad — caches the password to disk:</p>
<sample language="python">
import json
from pathlib import Path

def cache_creds(auth):
    Path(".kuauth-cache.json").write_text(
        json.dumps({"user": auth.username, "pw": auth._password})
    )
</sample>

<p>Good — caches only non-sensitive metadata:</p>
<sample language="python">
import json
from pathlib import Path

def cache_creds(auth):
    Path(".kuauth-cache.json").write_text(
        json.dumps({"user": auth.username})
    )
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>CWE-312: Cleartext Storage of Sensitive Information
(<a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/312.html">cwe.mitre.org/312</a>).</li>
<li>CWE-313: Cleartext Storage in a File or on Disk
(<a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/313.html">cwe.mitre.org/313</a>).</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
